# Line endings: LF for every text file, on every platform, in the repository and in
# every checkout. The tool behaves the same everywhere, and so must the bytes it is
# built from.
#
# Without a rule, git's `core.autocrlf` decides. `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git for
# Windows installer default, and under it a Windows checkout rewrites text files to
# CRLF; under `core.autocrlf=false`, a file that a tool saved with CRLF is committed
# with CRLF. The rules below take that choice away from the machine: `text` stores LF
# when a file is added, and `eol=lf` writes LF when it is checked out, whatever
# `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# There is no `eol=crlf` exception, and there should not be one. For a `text` file git
# always stores LF, and `eol=` only chooses what a checkout writes, so an exception
# could never store a file with CRLF. It would only make checkouts differ from the
# repository.

# The catch-all comes first, so a file type nobody listed below is still covered: a
# rule reaches exactly what it names, and a type it omits is left to `core.autocrlf`.
# `text=auto` leaves alone anything git detects as binary.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Declared, not detected. Every type this repository holds is named, so none of them
# depends on git guessing that it is text. Each is read by a tool (the compiler,
# MSBuild, GitHub Actions, the tests) or packed into the published package.
*.cs           text eol=lf
*.csproj       text eol=lf
*.props        text eol=lf
*.slnx         text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
/LICENSE       text eol=lf
/NOTICE        text eol=lf

# Binary types, declared so no text rule above can rewrite their bytes: a CR or LF
# byte inside an image is data. These lines stay last, because the last matching line
# wins.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
